{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat decisions/D021-gpu-budget-shim-qualified.md; sed -n '1,110p' cc/gpu_budget/README.md; rg -n 'NO_VMM|novmm|VMM' cc/build_engine/recipes/* cc/factory/releases* artifacts/x2/release* 2>/dev/null | head -20; ls artifacts/x3-shim/cuda/libgpubudget.so; ls -l /home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/*mixE4K*; sed -n '20,33p' artifacts/codexPL4/model-stats.json\"","aggregated_output":"# D021 — GPU budget shim (T-L) qualified on the RTX 5070 Ti; coverage on the real engine measured (x86-main, c2-resume)\nClaim category: infrastructure/fixture validation. NOT a hard device-memory boundary.\n\nEvidence: evidence/claude-code.jsonl task X3 (build, GPU test, engine probe); artifacts/x3-shim/cuda/{libgpubudget.so,test_budget,test-budget.jsonl}; artifacts/x3-shim/probe/nocap.jsonl.\nSource: commit 712fcd5 (Codex L final output; it finished AFTER the c1 snapshot, so the c1 tree held an earlier partial version).\n\n1. Build in campaign/cuda-tools:c1 succeeded; real CUDA test (budget 250 MiB) 39/39 PASS: runtime malloc/async/pitch/3D/managed, driver cuMemAlloc_v2/cuMemAllocAsync, meminfo virtualization, deny-leaves-accounting-unchanged.\n2. Engine probe (Build CC image b0 = sha256:cf383f82..., llama-completion, mixE4K, -ngl 99, default ctx, no cap, logging only): exactly 3 intercepted cudaMalloc: weights 778.7 MiB, KV 6144.0 MiB (default n_ctx = model max), compute 168.0 MiB; all freed at exit. Output correct (\"Paris\").\n3. NOT seen: ggml CUDA pool temporaries (VMM pool, cuMemCreate — uncovered by design), CUDA context/cuBLAS workspace, driver-internal allocations. Build CC NO_VMM variant (T-N) needed before temporaries are counted.\n\nGuarantee we can state: cooperative per-process cap on intercepted allocation APIs + virtual cudaMemGetInfo. It is bypassable (VMM, static cudart, cuGetProcAddress, other processes). Hard restriction: INCOMPLETE.\nDesign consequence: D018's ~700 MiB scaled budget is below the weights alone (779 MiB mixE4K); any budget experiment must declare n_ctx (KV dominates at default ctx) and count KV+compute, else the \"budget\" is meaningless. The budget should be expressed as {weights, kv@ctx, compute, uncounted-overhead measured by nvidia-smi delta}.\n\nSeparate gap found while resuming D020: job-8df3c7bb27's spec hashes the bf16 GGUF but every test loads model_file=mixE4K from the same directory -> the model actually used was not hash-verified by the lab. Lab fix: hash every model_file a test references (per-test input identity).\n# GPU allocation budget shim\n\n`libgpubudget.so` is a process-local `LD_PRELOAD` interposer for Linux and CUDA\n12.8. It caps **successful, intercepted allocation sizes**, aggregated across\nall devices and contexts in one process. It requires no driver changes, MPS,\nnetwork access, or privileged container options. It is an experiment control,\nnot a security boundary or a limit on physical VRAM reported by `nvidia-smi`.\n\nThe assignment's approximate scale is `128/500 * 2.67 GiB ≈ 700 MiB`.\nSet `GPU_BUDGET_MIB=700` for that allocation budget. If the 2.67 GB figure uses\ndecimal units instead, the corresponding result is approximately 652 MiB;\n700 MiB is the requested campaign approximation.\n\n## Build and test (orchestrator)\n\nRun these from the campaign root. These are instructions for the orchestrator;\nthe authoring agent did not run Docker. The source mount is read-only and all\ncompiler outputs go into the separate writable output mount:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p artifacts/codexL/cuda\ndocker run --rm --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/cc/gpu_budget:/src:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/artifacts/codexL/cuda:/out\" \\\n  campaign/cuda-tools:c1 bash /src/build.sh /out\n\ndocker run --rm --gpus all --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/cc/gpu_budget:/src:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/artifacts/codexL/cuda:/out\" \\\n  campaign/cuda-tools:c1 bash /src/run_test.sh /out\n```\n\n`build.sh [output-dir]` is the single build command inside the image. It uses\n`gcc` and CUDA headers for the shim, then `nvcc --cudart shared` for the test.\nThe shim depends only on the ELF loader, libc, and pthreads; it does not link\nagainst CUDA libraries. The test links the CUDA driver stub at build time,\nwithout embedding the stub directory in its runtime search path. At execution,\nthe NVIDIA container runtime must supply the real `libcuda.so.1`.\n\n`run_test.sh` pins budget=250, reserve=0, managed counting=1. It prints a\nPASS/FAIL table and exits nonzero if any check fails. It checks 3 × 100 MiB,\nthird-allocation OOM, free/reuse, exact memory-info values, async allocation,\nasync denial/free/reuse, pitched/3D/managed accounting, and direct synchronous\nand asynchronous driver allocation. It writes `test-budget.jsonl` in the output\ndirectory unless `GPU_BUDGET_LOG` is supplied. A GPU with managed memory and\nCUDA memory-pool support is required; unsupported async APIs are explicit test\nfailures, not silent passes.\n\n## Using with the engine\n\nSet `ENGINE_DIR` to the Build CC build output containing `bin/llama-cli` and\n`MODEL_DIR` to the model directory. For example:\n\n```bash\ndocker run --rm --gpus all \\\n  -v \"$ENGINE_DIR:/engine:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$MODEL_DIR:/models:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/artifacts/codexL/cuda:/budget:ro\" \\\n  -v \"$PWD/artifacts/codexL:/logs\" \\\n  -e LD_PRELOAD=/budget/libgpubudget.so \\\n  -e GPU_BUDGET_MIB=700 \\\n  -e GPU_BUDGET_LOG=/logs/engine-budget.jsonl \\\n  -e GGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=1 \\\n  campaign/cuda-tools:c1 \\\n  /engine/bin/llama-cli -m /models/model.gguf -ngl 999 -p 'Hello'\n```\n\n**VMM must actually be disabled in the engine build.** Source inspection of\nthis checkout found `GGML_CUDA_NO_VMM` is a compile-time setting, not a runtime\nenvironment switch. The environment setting above expresses the requested\ncampaign intent, but by itself has **no effect** on this revision. Build CC\nmust configure llama.cpp with `-DGGML_CUDA_NO_VMM=ON` and rebuild its CUDA backend.\n`ggml/src/ggml-cuda/common.cuh:232` and `CMakeLists.txt:147` guard VMM at compile\ntime; `ggml-cuda.cu:725` otherwise chooses the VMM pool when supported.\nCheck the Build CC `CMakeCache.txt` for `GGML_CUDA_NO_VMM:BOOL=ON`, the backend\nfeature report for `NO_VMM = 1`, and confirm budget-log `cudaMalloc` allocation\nevents while loading the model. A device capability line saying `VMM: yes`\nalone does not prove which pool was selected.\n\nThis requires dynamically linked CUDA runtime calls. The campaign image is\nspecified to link `libggml-cuda.so` against `libcudart.so.12`; verify the actual\nBuild CC artifact inside the image with `ldd /engine/bin/libggml-cuda.so` (adjust\nthe path if needed). Inspect `readelf -Ws` for undefined `cudaMalloc` and check\nthat the engine produces allocation events in the budget log. Static cudart,\nprivate symbol lookup, or direct driver function pointers can bypass preload.\nThe image and engine binary were unavailable for execution in this workspace,\nso their dynamic linkage and actual pool selection have not been GPU-verified.\n\nEngines using `cudaMemGetInfo`/`cuMemGetInfo_v2` or llama.cpp's\n`ggml_backend_dev_memory` see the synthetic budget and free balance. Fit logic\ncan reduce GPU layers or offload to CPU. Allocators that do not adapt receive\nOOM and may abort; llama.cpp pool growth, temporary buffers, and KV cache also\nconsume this budget. An exact physical-memory simulation is not implied.\n\n## Configuration and accounting\n\n| Variable | Behavior |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GPU_BUDGET_MIB` | Unsigned integer binary MiB. Absent: no cap and real memory-info values. `0`: no positive-size allocations. |\n| `GPU_BUDGET_RESERVE_MIB` | Default 0. Effective allocation capacity is `max(budget - reserve, 0)`. |\n| `GPU_BUDGET_COUNT_MANAGED` | `1` (default): charge requested managed bytes immediately, without tracking residency/prefetch. `0`: managed memory is outside the budget. |\n| `GPU_BUDGET_LOG` | Optional writable JSONL path, opened in append mode. All events are flushed immediately, including denials, plus a final summary at normal exit/unload. |\n\nConfiguration is read once on first intercepted outer call. Malformed integers,\noverflow, invalid managed settings, or an unopenable requested log terminate\nwith exit 125 rather than silently dropping the restriction. Each process has\nits own cap and must have its own log path for unambiguous records.\n\n`cudaMemGetInfo` and `cuMemGetInfo_v2` first invoke the real API so normal\ninitialization and invalid-argument/context errors are preserved. After success,\nartifacts/x3-shim/cuda/libgpubudget.so\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nobody nogroup 818259936 Nov  9 20:51 /home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf\n    \"mla_head_dim\": null,\n    \"mla_value_head_dim\": null,\n    \"expert_ffn_dim_source\": \"tensor shape\",\n    \"has_shared_experts\": false,\n    \"shape_order\": \"GGML ne[0..] (reverse of numpy/PyTorch)\",\n    \"tensors\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"output_norm.weight\",\n        \"ggml_type\": \"F32\",\n        \"shape\": [\n          1024\n        ],\n        \"bytes\": 4096,\n        \"role\": \"norm\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py')\ns=p.read_text().replace(\\\"pattern = '\"'^type_a=(q4_K\", \"pattern = r'\"'\"'^type_a=(q4_K\")\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat >> cc/host_calibrate/tests/test_calibrate.py <<'\"'PY'\n\n\ndef test_handoff_pipeline_synthetic_explicit(tmp_path):\n    # Synthetic mechanism test; benchmark formats separately have real captures above.\n    names=['nvidia','lscpu','CPU-MUL_MAT','CPU-MUL_MAT_ID','CUDA0-MUL_MAT','CUDA0-MUL_MAT_ID','h2d','bench-0','bench-1']\n    dense=(F/'cpu-perf-real.txt').read_text()\n    expert=(F/'cpu-expert-real.txt').read_text()\n    texts={'nvidia':'synthetic GPU', 'lscpu':(F/'lscpu-real.txt').read_text()}\n    for backend in ('CPU','CUDA0'):\n        texts[backend+'-MUL_MAT']='\\\\n'.join(dense.replace('q4_K',q) for q in c.TYPES)\n        texts[backend+'-MUL_MAT_ID']='\\\\n'.join(expert.replace('q4_K',q) for q in ('q4_K','q6_K','q8_0'))\n    texts['h2d']='\\\\n'.join(json.dumps(dict(direction='H2D',pinned=True,bytes=2**i*1048576,repeat=r,ms=1)) for i in range(9) for r in range(3))\n    bench=json.loads((F/'bench-real.json').read_text())\n    bench[0].update(n_gen=64,n_cpu_moe=0,samples_ts=[1000,1000])\n    texts['bench-0']=json.dumps(bench)\n    bench[0].update(n_cpu_moe=1,samples_ts=[500,500])\n    texts['bench-1']=json.dumps(bench)\n    for name,text in texts.items():\n        (tmp_path/f'{name}.stdout').write_text(text)\n        (tmp_path/f'{name}.status.json').write_text('{\\\"returncode\\\":0}')\n    stats=dict(n_layer=2,n_expert=4,n_expert_used=1,expert_bytes=4000,nonexpert_bytes=1000,\n               tensors=[dict(role='expert_ffn',layer=i,ggml_type='Q4_K',bytes=2000) for i in range(2)])\n    manifest=dict(image='synthetic',cpu_layers=[0,1],jobs=[{'name':n} for n in names])\n    result=c.parse_capture(manifest,tmp_path,stats)\n    terms=result['handoff_terms']\n    assert result['constants']['handoff_us']==pytest.approx(1000-terms['delta_cpu_us']-terms['delta_gpu_us'])\n    assert terms['delta_gpu_us']<0\n    (tmp_path/'h2d.status.json').write_text('{\\\"returncode\\\":124}')\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError,match='h2d failed'): c.parse_capture(manifest,tmp_path,stats)\nPY\ncat > cc/host_calibrate/README.md <<'MD'\n# Host calibration (T-CAL)\n\nOffline planner/parser and operator-run probes for llama.cpp pin\n\"'`31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37`. All generated files belong under\n`artifacts/codexCAL/`. No Docker/GPU calls were executed by the author.\nExisting planner files and their shared in-progress changes were left untouched.\n\n`calibrate.py plan` emits **a JSON list of Docker argv**, plus a named manifest.\n`calibrate.py parse` separately reads captured stdout/status and emits\n`host-calibration/1`: decimal GB/s constants, raw samples and output text,\nimage/pin provenance, CPU/GPU identity text, observed uncertainty and caveats.\nNo fabricated calibration JSON is supplied as a host measurement.\n\n## Exact operator commands (Opus, GPU host)\n\nFrom the campaign root, set `RUN_REF` to the immutable **release engine** image\nreference. It must contain `/app/test-backend-ops`, `/app/llama-bench`, `timeout`,\n`nvidia-smi` and `lscpu`; missing tools fail explicitly. Do not silently substitute\nanother engine image. The pinned factory recipe excludes tests by default, so\ncheck the release packaging before running. The tools image is only for `nvcc`.\n\n```bash\nexport PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1\nexport RUN_REF='\"'sha256:REPLACE_WITH_RELEASE_ENGINE_RUN_REF'\nexport MODEL='/home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf'\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py plan \\\\\n  --image \\\"\"'$RUN_REF\" --model \"$MODEL\" --cpu-layers 0 12 '\"\\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/gpu > artifacts/codexCAL/command-plan.json\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/run_plan.py artifacts/codexCAL/gpu\npython3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n# Actual first-K layer tensor headers; normalized stats already used by T-PL4.\ns = json.loads(Path('artifacts/codexPL4/model-stats.json').read_text())['mixE4K']\nPath('artifacts/codexCAL/model-stats.json').write_text(json.dumps(s, indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nPY\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py parse \\\\\n  --capture artifacts/codexCAL/gpu --stats artifacts/codexCAL/model-stats.json \\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/host-calibration.json\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/apply.py artifacts/codexCAL/host-calibration.json \\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/planner-calibration.json\n\"'```\n\nThe supplied stats must describe the supplied model, including the exact\nfirst-K expert types and byte totals. Use another pair of K values only with\nmatching actual tensor stats. Do not use a large production model: planning\nrejects model files over 1 GiB. The provided Granite mixE4K is ~780 MiB;\nbench generates only 64 tokens with batch/ubatch 64, leaving more than 1 GiB\nfor small KV, compute and CUDA overhead within the 2 GiB allocation envelope.\nThe source shapes use at most ~204 MiB weight banks plus ~768 MiB transient\nfloat initialization; dense screens use <60 MiB weights. H2D explicitly\nallocates at most 256 MiB host + 256 MiB device. Containers have a 2 GiB host\ncgroup cap, no swap allowance beyond that cap, read-only rootfs, no network,\nread-only inputs, and only the build artifact directory mounted writable.\nThese are bounded workloads, not a claim of a hard physical VRAM partition\n(the D021 interposer also cannot account for driver/context allocations).\nUse the supplied small model; arbitrary architectures of the same file size\nhave not had their compute allocations qualified.\n\nEach probe has an in-container 25 s timeout with a 1 s kill grace. The optional\nrunner has an outer 29 s deadline plus a 1 s container-removal deadline; it\nrecords failures and stops rather than parsing partial results. All probes run\nserially. Expected warm-path total is about 30–60 s: 16 matrix cases at at\nleast 1 s each, two tg64/r2 benches, a short copy sweep and build/startup.\nCold image pulls/model page faults and slow CPUs can exceed 60 s; this is a\nbudget target, not a measured runtime. No global 60 s deadline truncates data.\nNo sudo, host settings, package installation, or memory-filling screens.\n\nThe exact build argv is also in the emitted plan:\n`docker run ... campaign/cuda-tools:c1 timeout -k 1s 25s nvcc -O2 /src/h2d.cu -o /out/h2d`.\nThe CUDA source uses a pinned-memory `cudaMemcpyAsync` sweep of 1, 2, 4, 8,\n16, 32, 64, 128, 256 MiB, a warmup and three repeats with eight copies per\nCUDA-event timing window. `ms` is time per copy. The constant is the median\nof the nine samples at 64–256 MiB; all 27 samples remain in the report.\n\n## Actual pinned output and bandwidth semantics\n\n`artifacts/codexCAL/test-backend-ops-pinned.cpp` was extracted with\n`git -C ref/llama.cpp show 31288c9c6:tests/test-backend-ops.cpp`.\nRelevant source: `print_perf_console` lines 987–1027; `eval_perf` lines\n1580–1719; cases lines 12303–12335; CLI lines 13059–13123.\nThe literal printf statements include:\n\n```cpp\nprintf(\"    %8d runs - %8.2f us/run - \", result.n_runs, result.time_us);\nprintf(\"%s/run - '\"\\\\033[1;34m%sS\\\\033[0m\\\", format_flops(op_flops_per_run).c_str(),\n       format_flops(result.flops).c_str());\nprintf(\\\"%8zu kB/run - \\\\033[1;34m%7.2f GB/s\\\\033[0m\\\", result.memory_kb, result.bandwidth_gb_s);\n\"'```\n\n**The requested MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID do not print GB/s at this pin.** They\nhave nonzero `op_flops`, print FLOP/s, and even SQL'\"'s \"'`bandwidth_gb_s` is zero.\nCSV omits performance fields entirely. Inventing a matrix GB/s output format\nwould be wrong. The parser accepts the actual ANSI console rows and calculates\nactive compressed weight bytes / measured microseconds / 1000, in decimal GB/s.\nIt also extracts literal GB/s when present; upstream'\"'s label actually means\nGiB/s (source divides by 1024 cubed), so the report retains both original and\nconverted decimal values. That literal branch is **UNVERIFIED** by a real capture.\n\nDense screens use m4096/k14336/n1; expert screens use m768/k2048/n1,\n128 experts/8 selected. Quant blocks (bytes per block): Q2_K 84/256,\nQ3_K 110/256, Q4_K 144/256, Q6_K 210/256, Q8_0 34/32.\nExpert bandwidth counts selected experts, not the entire bank.\nThe pin's expert perf cases omit Q2_K/Q3_K; those types explicitly use dense\nCPU GEMV as a proxy. Q4_K/Q6_K/Q8_0 CPU constants use expert results. GPU\nconstant uses Q4_K dense results; other GPU types and expert screens are raw\ndiagnostics. These operations include dispatch and dequantization, and repeated\nweights may hit cache. Do not label them hardware DRAM bandwidth or validated\nwhole-model coefficients. Raw H2D excludes the cache dispatch captured in T-PL4's\nfitted H2D term (D041); cache miss latency remains unmeasured.\n\n## Handoff algebra and uncertainty\n\nUsing \"'`decode_model.traffic` on actual tensor headers at K0 and K1:\n\n```text\nt(K) = G(K)/(1000*BWgpu) + sum_q Cq(K)/(1000*BWcpu,q) + K*h\nh = [median(t1) - median(t0)\n     - sum_q (Cq1-Cq0)/(1000*BWcpu,q)\n     - (G1-G0)/(1000*BWgpu)] / (K1-K0)\n```\n\nTimes are microseconds per token (`1e6 / samples_ts`), bytes are active\nbytes/token. The GPU delta is negative as experts move to CPU. Omitting that\nterm misattributes removed GPU work to handoff. Both bench runs use the same\nimage, model, batch, threads and token count. The implementation takes the\nmedian of pairwise repeat residuals (equivalent for the default two repeats).\nNegative handoff fails calibration rather than being silently clamped.\nIt indicates an incompatible/noisy bandwidth model or insufficient signal.\n\nUncertainty includes count, observed range and sample standard deviation for\nbandwidth, plus pairwise residual handoff ranges. Single case estimates have\nnull standard deviation. Pairwise residuals are correlated, not independent\nmeasurements or confidence intervals. Systematic model error, shared-host load,\nplacement-dependent numeric routes, attention and cache residency are not\nbounded by those ranges. This is a per-host initial screen, not D039/D041\ncross-model validation. Failures and unavailable identity information do not\nbecome zero measurements (consistent with resource_observe'\"'s contract).\n\n## Apply to planner without changing planner files\n\n\"'`apply.py` writes a complete independent calibration object with\n`physical_decode.constants` replaced. It drops stale CPU screen values and\nclears old physical fit evaluation/fold summaries. It retains historical memory,\nprefill, quality and host applicability rules; these were not recalibrated.\nConsume it with the planner'\"'s existing **Python API** \"'`cal=` argument:\n\n```python\nimport json, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '\"'cc/moe_planner')\nimport plan\ncal = json.load(open('artifacts/codexCAL/planner-calibration.json'))\nresult = plan.plan(stats, host, budget, cal=cal, allow_extrapolation=True)\n\"'```\n\nOr use `decode_model.predict(stats, family, coord,\ncal['\"'physical_decode']['constants'])\"'` directly. There is no existing planner\nCLI calibration-file flag. The API suffices, so no write outside the two\nassignment roots or new planner hook was needed. `--out` checks resolve symlinks\nand prohibit escaping `artifacts/codexCAL/`.\n\n## Verification status and tests\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider '\"\\\\\n  cc/host_calibrate/tests --basetemp=artifacts/codexCAL/pytest-tmp\n\"'```\n\n* **Verified real formats:** MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID console timing parsers\n  (actual CPU outputs captured here from the existing pinned-source build);\n  llama-bench JSON parser (copied unchanged T-PL4 tg32 CPU output, tested with\n  n_gen32); lscpu identity text (actual capture here).\n* **UNVERIFIED:** CUDA backend execution/timing, literal GB/s branch, H2D\n  CUDA compilation/output parser (only explicit synthetic sweep test),\n  nvidia-smi successful device identity (retained verbatim), GPU tg64 paired\n  handoff estimation and total wall-clock target. The pipeline/algebra test\n  uses explicitly synthetic times; it does not claim a real handoff sample.\n\nReal fixtures are in `tests/fixtures/`, raw new captures/stderr and source in\n`artifacts/codexCAL/`; `capture-provenance.json` records paths, commands and\nhashes. Captured CPU numbers are shared-host observations used to validate\nformat, not a GPU host-calibration result. No synthetic success is passed off\nas an instrument measurement.\nMD\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cc/host_calibrate/tests --basetemp=artifacts/codexCAL/pytest-tmp > artifacts/codexCAL/pytest.txt 2>&1\ncat artifacts/codexCAL/pytest.txt'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 -B - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py')\ns=p.read_text().replace(\\\"pattern = '\"'^type_a=(q4_K\", \"pattern = r'\"'\"'^type_a=(q4_K\")\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ncat >> cc/host_calibrate/tests/test_calibrate.py <<'\"'PY'\n\n\ndef test_handoff_pipeline_synthetic_explicit(tmp_path):\n    # Synthetic mechanism test; benchmark formats separately have real captures above.\n    names=['nvidia','lscpu','CPU-MUL_MAT','CPU-MUL_MAT_ID','CUDA0-MUL_MAT','CUDA0-MUL_MAT_ID','h2d','bench-0','bench-1']\n    dense=(F/'cpu-perf-real.txt').read_text()\n    expert=(F/'cpu-expert-real.txt').read_text()\n    texts={'nvidia':'synthetic GPU', 'lscpu':(F/'lscpu-real.txt').read_text()}\n    for backend in ('CPU','CUDA0'):\n        texts[backend+'-MUL_MAT']='\\\\n'.join(dense.replace('q4_K',q) for q in c.TYPES)\n        texts[backend+'-MUL_MAT_ID']='\\\\n'.join(expert.replace('q4_K',q) for q in ('q4_K','q6_K','q8_0'))\n    texts['h2d']='\\\\n'.join(json.dumps(dict(direction='H2D',pinned=True,bytes=2**i*1048576,repeat=r,ms=1)) for i in range(9) for r in range(3))\n    bench=json.loads((F/'bench-real.json').read_text())\n    bench[0].update(n_gen=64,n_cpu_moe=0,samples_ts=[1000,1000])\n    texts['bench-0']=json.dumps(bench)\n    bench[0].update(n_cpu_moe=1,samples_ts=[500,500])\n    texts['bench-1']=json.dumps(bench)\n    for name,text in texts.items():\n        (tmp_path/f'{name}.stdout').write_text(text)\n        (tmp_path/f'{name}.status.json').write_text('{\\\"returncode\\\":0}')\n    stats=dict(n_layer=2,n_expert=4,n_expert_used=1,expert_bytes=4000,nonexpert_bytes=1000,\n               tensors=[dict(role='expert_ffn',layer=i,ggml_type='Q4_K',bytes=2000) for i in range(2)])\n    manifest=dict(image='synthetic',cpu_layers=[0,1],jobs=[{'name':n} for n in names])\n    result=c.parse_capture(manifest,tmp_path,stats)\n    terms=result['handoff_terms']\n    assert result['constants']['handoff_us']==pytest.approx(1000-terms['delta_cpu_us']-terms['delta_gpu_us'])\n    assert terms['delta_gpu_us']<0\n    (tmp_path/'h2d.status.json').write_text('{\\\"returncode\\\":124}')\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError,match='h2d failed'): c.parse_capture(manifest,tmp_path,stats)\nPY\ncat > cc/host_calibrate/README.md <<'MD'\n# Host calibration (T-CAL)\n\nOffline planner/parser and operator-run probes for llama.cpp pin\n\"'`31288c9c66e59377f7788d920678a565bb89dd37`. All generated files belong under\n`artifacts/codexCAL/`. No Docker/GPU calls were executed by the author.\nExisting planner files and their shared in-progress changes were left untouched.\n\n`calibrate.py plan` emits **a JSON list of Docker argv**, plus a named manifest.\n`calibrate.py parse` separately reads captured stdout/status and emits\n`host-calibration/1`: decimal GB/s constants, raw samples and output text,\nimage/pin provenance, CPU/GPU identity text, observed uncertainty and caveats.\nNo fabricated calibration JSON is supplied as a host measurement.\n\n## Exact operator commands (Opus, GPU host)\n\nFrom the campaign root, set `RUN_REF` to the immutable **release engine** image\nreference. It must contain `/app/test-backend-ops`, `/app/llama-bench`, `timeout`,\n`nvidia-smi` and `lscpu`; missing tools fail explicitly. Do not silently substitute\nanother engine image. The pinned factory recipe excludes tests by default, so\ncheck the release packaging before running. The tools image is only for `nvcc`.\n\n```bash\nexport PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1\nexport RUN_REF='\"'sha256:REPLACE_WITH_RELEASE_ENGINE_RUN_REF'\nexport MODEL='/home/muk/models/campaign/gguf/granite-3.1-1b-a400m-instruct-mixE4K.gguf'\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py plan \\\\\n  --image \\\"\"'$RUN_REF\" --model \"$MODEL\" --cpu-layers 0 12 '\"\\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/gpu > artifacts/codexCAL/command-plan.json\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/run_plan.py artifacts/codexCAL/gpu\npython3 -B - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n# Actual first-K layer tensor headers; normalized stats already used by T-PL4.\ns = json.loads(Path('artifacts/codexPL4/model-stats.json').read_text())['mixE4K']\nPath('artifacts/codexCAL/model-stats.json').write_text(json.dumps(s, indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nPY\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/calibrate.py parse \\\\\n  --capture artifacts/codexCAL/gpu --stats artifacts/codexCAL/model-stats.json \\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/host-calibration.json\npython3 -B cc/host_calibrate/apply.py artifacts/codexCAL/host-calibration.json \\\\\n  --out artifacts/codexCAL/planner-calibration.json\n\"'```\n\nThe supplied stats must describe the supplied model, including the exact\nfirst-K expert types and byte totals. Use another pair of K values only with\nmatching actual tensor stats. Do not use a large production model: planning\nrejects model files over 1 GiB. The provided Granite mixE4K is ~780 MiB;\nbench generates only 64 tokens with batch/ubatch 64, leaving more than 1 GiB\nfor small KV, compute and CUDA overhead within the 2 GiB allocation envelope.\nThe source shapes use at most ~204 MiB weight banks plus ~768 MiB transient\nfloat initialization; dense screens use <60 MiB weights. H2D explicitly\nallocates at most 256 MiB host + 256 MiB device. Containers have a 2 GiB host\ncgroup cap, no swap allowance beyond that cap, read-only rootfs, no network,\nread-only inputs, and only the build artifact directory mounted writable.\nThese are bounded workloads, not a claim of a hard physical VRAM partition\n(the D021 interposer also cannot account for driver/context allocations).\nUse the supplied small model; arbitrary architectures of the same file size\nhave not had their compute allocations qualified.\n\nEach probe has an in-container 25 s timeout with a 1 s kill grace. The optional\nrunner has an outer 29 s deadline plus a 1 s container-removal deadline; it\nrecords failures and stops rather than parsing partial results. All probes run\nserially. Expected warm-path total is about 30–60 s: 16 matrix cases at at\nleast 1 s each, two tg64/r2 benches, a short copy sweep and build/startup.\nCold image pulls/model page faults and slow CPUs can exceed 60 s; this is a\nbudget target, not a measured runtime. No global 60 s deadline truncates data.\nNo sudo, host settings, package installation, or memory-filling screens.\n\nThe exact build argv is also in the emitted plan:\n`docker run ... campaign/cuda-tools:c1 timeout -k 1s 25s nvcc -O2 /src/h2d.cu -o /out/h2d`.\nThe CUDA source uses a pinned-memory `cudaMemcpyAsync` sweep of 1, 2, 4, 8,\n16, 32, 64, 128, 256 MiB, a warmup and three repeats with eight copies per\nCUDA-event timing window. `ms` is time per copy. The constant is the median\nof the nine samples at 64–256 MiB; all 27 samples remain in the report.\n\n## Actual pinned output and bandwidth semantics\n\n`artifacts/codexCAL/test-backend-ops-pinned.cpp` was extracted with\n`git -C ref/llama.cpp show 31288c9c6:tests/test-backend-ops.cpp`.\nRelevant source: `print_perf_console` lines 987–1027; `eval_perf` lines\n1580–1719; cases lines 12303–12335; CLI lines 13059–13123.\nThe literal printf statements include:\n\n```cpp\nprintf(\"    %8d runs - %8.2f us/run - \", result.n_runs, result.time_us);\nprintf(\"%s/run - '\"\\\\033[1;34m%sS\\\\033[0m\\\", format_flops(op_flops_per_run).c_str(),\n       format_flops(result.flops).c_str());\nprintf(\\\"%8zu kB/run - \\\\033[1;34m%7.2f GB/s\\\\033[0m\\\", result.memory_kb, result.bandwidth_gb_s);\n\"'```\n\n**The requested MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID do not print GB/s at this pin.** They\nhave nonzero `op_flops`, print FLOP/s, and even SQL'\"'s \"'`bandwidth_gb_s` is zero.\nCSV omits performance fields entirely. Inventing a matrix GB/s output format\nwould be wrong. The parser accepts the actual ANSI console rows and calculates\nactive compressed weight bytes / measured microseconds / 1000, in decimal GB/s.\nIt also extracts literal GB/s when present; upstream'\"'s label actually means\nGiB/s (source divides by 1024 cubed), so the report retains both original and\nconverted decimal values. That literal branch is **UNVERIFIED** by a real capture.\n\nDense screens use m4096/k14336/n1; expert screens use m768/k2048/n1,\n128 experts/8 selected. Quant blocks (bytes per block): Q2_K 84/256,\nQ3_K 110/256, Q4_K 144/256, Q6_K 210/256, Q8_0 34/32.\nExpert bandwidth counts selected experts, not the entire bank.\nThe pin's expert perf cases omit Q2_K/Q3_K; those types explicitly use dense\nCPU GEMV as a proxy. Q4_K/Q6_K/Q8_0 CPU constants use expert results. GPU\nconstant uses Q4_K dense results; other GPU types and expert screens are raw\ndiagnostics. These operations include dispatch and dequantization, and repeated\nweights may hit cache. Do not label them hardware DRAM bandwidth or validated\nwhole-model coefficients. Raw H2D excludes the cache dispatch captured in T-PL4's\nfitted H2D term (D041); cache miss latency remains unmeasured.\n\n## Handoff algebra and uncertainty\n\nUsing \"'`decode_model.traffic` on actual tensor headers at K0 and K1:\n\n```text\nt(K) = G(K)/(1000*BWgpu) + sum_q Cq(K)/(1000*BWcpu,q) + K*h\nh = [median(t1) - median(t0)\n     - sum_q (Cq1-Cq0)/(1000*BWcpu,q)\n     - (G1-G0)/(1000*BWgpu)] / (K1-K0)\n```\n\nTimes are microseconds per token (`1e6 / samples_ts`), bytes are active\nbytes/token. The GPU delta is negative as experts move to CPU. Omitting that\nterm misattributes removed GPU work to handoff. Both bench runs use the same\nimage, model, batch, threads and token count. The implementation takes the\nmedian of pairwise repeat residuals (equivalent for the default two repeats).\nNegative handoff fails calibration rather than being silently clamped.\nIt indicates an incompatible/noisy bandwidth model or insufficient signal.\n\nUncertainty includes count, observed range and sample standard deviation for\nbandwidth, plus pairwise residual handoff ranges. Single case estimates have\nnull standard deviation. Pairwise residuals are correlated, not independent\nmeasurements or confidence intervals. Systematic model error, shared-host load,\nplacement-dependent numeric routes, attention and cache residency are not\nbounded by those ranges. This is a per-host initial screen, not D039/D041\ncross-model validation. Failures and unavailable identity information do not\nbecome zero measurements (consistent with resource_observe'\"'s contract).\n\n## Apply to planner without changing planner files\n\n\"'`apply.py` writes a complete independent calibration object with\n`physical_decode.constants` replaced. It drops stale CPU screen values and\nclears old physical fit evaluation/fold summaries. It retains historical memory,\nprefill, quality and host applicability rules; these were not recalibrated.\nConsume it with the planner'\"'s existing **Python API** \"'`cal=` argument:\n\n```python\nimport json, sys\nsys.path.insert(0, '\"'cc/moe_planner')\nimport plan\ncal = json.load(open('artifacts/codexCAL/planner-calibration.json'))\nresult = plan.plan(stats, host, budget, cal=cal, allow_extrapolation=True)\n\"'```\n\nOr use `decode_model.predict(stats, family, coord,\ncal['\"'physical_decode']['constants'])\"'` directly. There is no existing planner\nCLI calibration-file flag. The API suffices, so no write outside the two\nassignment roots or new planner hook was needed. `--out` checks resolve symlinks\nand prohibit escaping `artifacts/codexCAL/`.\n\n## Verification status and tests\n\n```bash\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider '\"\\\\\n  cc/host_calibrate/tests --basetemp=artifacts/codexCAL/pytest-tmp\n\"'```\n\n* **Verified real formats:** MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID console timing parsers\n  (actual CPU outputs captured here from the existing pinned-source build);\n  llama-bench JSON parser (copied unchanged T-PL4 tg32 CPU output, tested with\n  n_gen32); lscpu identity text (actual capture here).\n* **UNVERIFIED:** CUDA backend execution/timing, literal GB/s branch, H2D\n  CUDA compilation/output parser (only explicit synthetic sweep test),\n  nvidia-smi successful device identity (retained verbatim), GPU tg64 paired\n  handoff estimation and total wall-clock target. The pipeline/algebra test\n  uses explicitly synthetic times; it does not claim a real handoff sample.\n\nReal fixtures are in `tests/fixtures/`, raw new captures/stderr and source in\n`artifacts/codexCAL/`; `capture-provenance.json` records paths, commands and\nhashes. Captured CPU numbers are shared-host observations used to validate\nformat, not a GPU host-calibration result. No synthetic success is passed off\nas an instrument measurement.\nMD\nPYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 -B -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider cc/host_calibrate/tests --basetemp=artifacts/codexCAL/pytest-tmp > artifacts/codexCAL/pytest.txt 2>&1\ncat artifacts/codexCAL/pytest.txt'","aggregated_output":"..............                                                           [100%]\n14 passed in 0.04s\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
